Starting with RCC (Reporter Code Count) files means you are working with NanoString nCounter data. To analyze multiple nCounter datasets together in R, the cleanest approach is using the NanoTube Bioconductor package alongside limma.

Step 1: Load and Align Your RCC Files

Place your RCC files (or subdirectories per dataset) into folders and create a combined sample sheet (samples.csv) containing FileName, SampleID, Condition, and Dataset (batch).
r
library(NanoTube)
library(limma)
library(dplyr)

# Read RCC files from multiple directories or a single combined list
# NanoTube automatically checks RLF (CodeSet) compatibility across files
rcc_files <- list.files("path/to/all_rcc_folders", pattern = "\\.RCC$", recursive = TRUE, full.names = TRUE)
sample_info <- read.csv("samples.csv")

# Import raw counts and perform basic QC
dat <- processNanostringData(
  nsFiles = rcc_files,
  sampleTab = sample_info,
  idCol = "SampleID",
  groupCol = "Condition",
  normalization = "none" # Keep raw first so we can apply cross-dataset normalization
)
Use code with caution.
• If CodeSets (RLFs) differ across datasets. processNanostringData will throw an error if probe lists do not match exactly. In that case, read each dataset separately using read_rcc() from the nanostringr package, intersect the shared endogenous, housekeeping, and negative control probes, and merge the count matrices manually before proceeding.

Step 2: Cross-Dataset Normalization

Choose one of two paths depending on whether your datasets share technical replicates (the exact same RNA sample run in both datasets).

Option A: You HAVE technical replicates (Recommended: RUV-III)

RUV-III uses negative controls, housekeeping genes, and replicate samples across batches to remove technical variation.
r
# Define replicate matrix: rows = samples, cols = unique biological specimens
# Technical replicates from different datasets share the same column with a 1
rep_mat <- ruv::replicate.matrix(sample_info$Unique_Biological_Sample_ID)

# Run RUV-III normalization via NanoTube
dat_norm <- normalize_nanostring(
  nsData = dat,
  method = "RUVIII",
  repMat = rep_mat,
  k = 2 # Number of unwanted variation factors to remove (inspect PCA to tune)
)
Use code with caution.

Option B: You DO NOT have technical replicates (Standard Scaling + Batch Modeling)

When no shared samples exist across datasets, normalize within each sample using positive controls and housekeeping genes, then handle the dataset batch effect inside your statistical model.
r
# Standard positive control + housekeeping normalization
dat_norm <- processNanostringData(
  nsFiles = rcc_files,
  sampleTab = sample_info,
  idCol = "SampleID",
  groupCol = "Condition",
  normalization = "nSolver"
)
Use code with caution.

Step 3: Verify Batch Removal (QC Visualization)

Check whether samples cluster by Condition rather than Dataset using Principal Component Analysis (PCA).
r
expr_mat <- Biobase::exprs(dat_norm)

# For Option B (visualizing without batch effects), regress out Dataset for plotting ONLY
expr_for_plot <- removeBatchEffect(expr_mat, batch = sample_info$Dataset)

pca <- prcomp(t(expr_for_plot), scale. = TRUE)
plot(pca$x[, 1], pca$x[, 2], col = as.factor(sample_info$Dataset), pch = 19,
     xlab = "PC1", ylab = "PC2", main = "PCA Colored by Dataset")
Use code with caution.

Step 4: Differential Expression Across Datasets

Run linear modeling with limma on your normalized expression matrix (expr_mat, not expr_for_plot).
r
# If you used Option A (RUV-III), batch is already removed:
design <- model.matrix(~ 0 + Condition, data = sample_info)

# If you used Option B (no replicates), include Dataset as a covariate:
design <- model.matrix(~ 0 + Condition + Dataset, data = sample_info)

fit <- lmFit(expr_mat, design)
contrast_matrix <- makeContrasts(Treated_vs_Control = ConditionTreated - ConditionControl, levels = design)
fit2 <- contrasts.fit(fit, contrast_matrix)
fit2 <- eBayes(fit2)

results <- topTable(fit2, number = Inf, adjust.method = "BH")
Use code with caution.
To tailor this script to your exact setup, let me know:
• Whether all your RCC files use the exact same RLF (CodeSet) or different panel versions
• Whether you have technical replicates or control samples shared across datasets
